bf16 GEMM, NPU vs CPU, at shapes beyond the single 512x512x512 anchor in
mlir_aie_bf16_matmul_npu.log -- the first head-to-head NPU-vs-CPU bf16/fp32 matmul
comparison in this project (every other CPU number here is int8 QDQ conv).
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, both sides one sitting.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
attention_bf16/README.md closed MobileViT-scale fused attention as a definitive loss
(71-240x) and did the math on why: even a *perfect* aie::mmul kernel needs the op to be
~20x bigger than mobile-vision attention before compute time (at the 895 GFLOPS anchor)
outruns the ~617us dispatch floor. That points at LLM-scale GEMM, not mobile vision. But
no CPU bf16/fp32 GEMM baseline existed anywhere in this repo to compare against at any
size -- so "895 GFLOPS is good" was never checked against what this machine's own CPU
does at bf16 matmul. It is checked here.

  NPU : whole_array.py (~/mlir-aie/programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/
        whole_array), --n-aie-cols 4 --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out bf16, ironenv
  CPU : kernels/bf16_matmul_sweep/cpu_matmul_sweep.py, torch 2.4.1+cpu, resnet_env

Every NPU number below is a verified PASS (whole_array.py's own A@B correctness check
against numpy), not just timed.

RESULTS -- hardware bracket (NPU) vs mean wall time (CPU), same MxKxN shape
=============================================================================
      MxKxN         NPU GFLOPS   CPU bf16 GFLOPS   ratio    winner
------------------------------------------------------------------------
   512x512x512          895.08           1100.6     0.81x   CPU
  512x512x1024         1339.03           1134.5     1.18x   NPU
  512x512x2048         1675.84           1146.6     1.46x   NPU
 512x2048x2048         1645.62           1124.0     1.46x   NPU
1024x1024x1024         2072.54           1161.2     1.78x   NPU
2048x2048x2048         1791.90           1197.5     1.50x   NPU
2048x2048x4096         1842.70           1323.2     1.39x   NPU
4096x2048x2048         1846.96           1247.7     1.48x   NPU
   K >= 3072 (any M, N)         FAIL -- output does not match A@B (see below)

CPU fp32 numbers (for reference, not the comparison -- bf16 is the like-for-like
dtype): 512-580 GFLOPS at all shapes tested, roughly half the bf16 rate, consistent
with AVX-512 fp32 vs bf16 throughput on this Zen4 core.

THE FINDING
===========
**The first genuine NPU win measured anywhere in this project.** At the single shape
this project had previously measured (512x512x512), the NPU's 895 GFLOPS actually
*loses* to plain CPU torch bf16 matmul (1100.6 GFLOPS) on this specific machine (Ryzen
7 8700G, no discrete GPU) -- so the "895 GFLOPS is a strength" framing in
mlir_aie_bf16_matmul_npu.log was incomplete without this comparison. But GFLOPS is not
flat with size on either side, and they diverge: NPU throughput keeps climbing with M/N
(895 -> ~1800-2070 GFLOPS across the shapes tested) while CPU bf16 throughput is close
to flat (1100-1360 GFLOPS across the same range, mild upward drift from cache effects).
The crossover is around N=1024 at M=K=512: NPU wins from there on, by 1.2x-1.8x, up to
the K limit below.

K >= 3072 FAILS CORRECTNESS -- A REAL, UNDIAGNOSED LIMIT, NOT A TYPO
=====================================================================
M=4096 alone (K=N=2048) passes. N=4096 alone (M=K=2048) passes. K=3072 or K=4096 fails
regardless of M/N (tested M=K=N=4096, M=2048/K=4096/N=2048, M=2048/K=3072/N=2048 --
all FAIL; only the K dimension was implicated in every failing combination tested).
AIE2's aie::mmul accumulates bf16 x bf16 -> fp32 natively, so this does not look like
ordinary bf16 rounding drift; more likely a tiling/indexing limit in this in-tree
whole_array design specific to the K (reduction) loop's tile count at this scale. NOT
diagnosed further this session -- upstream example code, out of scope for this pass.
Flagged as an open item: the practical bf16 GEMM range on this hardware, with this
design, is K <= ~2048.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE "FIND THE NPU'S NICHE" QUESTION
=========================================================
This is the answer the project was looking for after conv (12.75x loss, even at
ResNet50's real shape, bottleneck_w56_npu.log) and mobile-vision attention (71-240x
loss, attention_bf16/README.md) both closed against the NPU. Large bf16 GEMM -- M/N in
the 1024-4096 range, K up to ~2048 -- is a real, measured NPU strength on this specific
machine, not a marketing GFLOPS number. This is exactly the "LLM-scale, not mobile
vision" shape attention_bf16/README.md's own math predicted would be needed. It does
NOT mean attention itself would win yet -- attention adds softmax and two matmuls with
data-dependent shapes, not a single large GEMM, and the K>=3072 ceiling would bite an
attention block with head_dim x seq_len anywhere near LLM scale (seq_len >= 2048 was
named as the threshold that would matter). Next step, not done this session: either
diagnose the K>=3072 failure (it may be a small tiling fix, unlike conv2dk3/conv2dk1's
register-spill class of bug) or build a fused attention/FFN block that stays within the
verified K<=2048 envelope and re-measure end to end.

Local mlir-aie checkout only where whole_array.py was invoked directly (no local edits
made to it this session, unlike bottleneck.py). cpu_matmul_sweep.py is new, committed
to this repo at kernels/bf16_matmul_sweep/.
